  "lane": "independent structural-habitat index: z(log1p detrended-elevation slope) + z(log1p TMI horizontal gradient) - z(tilt-angle derivative) + 0.5*z(|isostatic-gravity horizontal gradient|), rank-transformed to a within-footprint quantile, discounted by (1 - exp(-d_catalogue / 3 px)) and zeroed within 100 m of a mapped fault; dots placed by lazy-greedy (CELF) maximisation of the expected distance-weighted Tversky index with K-hat = 35,000",
  "hypothesis": "the scored faults are faults the USGS/INGENIOUS catalogue does NOT contain, so a useful density must rank under-mapped ground rather than ground that looks like mapped faults. Measured support: at 10 km block scale the dot density of the best-scoring published file correlates with detrended-elevation slope (Spearman +0.46), detrended elevation (+0.37) and TMI horizontal gradient (+0.36), and anti-correlates with the tilt derivative (-0.18); every published file scoring >= 0.245 has a median dot-to-catalogue distance of 15.0-19.7 px, while every file scoring <= 0.15 has 4.1-5.8 px.",
  "mechanism": "four of the 19 GeoDAWN bands are z-scored inside the footprint and summed with the weights above; no model is trained and no published raster is an input, so the index is a closed-form geophysical surface. The rank transform is required because the raw classifier-style score of the earlier supervised lane was far more concentrated than any published submission behaves; the rank keeps the ordering and leaves the emitted territory's extent under explicit control of eta.",
  "named_non_fault_mimic": "range-front scarps and alluvial-fan edges: the detrended-elevation slope term fires on any topographic break, including purely erosional or depositional fronts. The tilt-derivative term also fires on flight-line striping and on lithologic contacts that are not faults.",
